            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Scene: Day 3 After Launch, $200 Ad Spend with Almost No Sales</h2>
<p>A 3C accessories team in Shenzhen launched a Bluetooth earphone on TikTok Shop Malaysia. </p>
<p>Priced at $12.9, with a first-week ad budget of $200. Operator Xiao Li set up 3 ads with confidence—but after 3 days:</p>
<ul>
<li>12,000 total impressions</li>
<li>CTR at 0.6%, far below category average of 1.8%</li>
<li>CVR at 0.7%, almost no one purchased</li>
<li>$182 ad spend, GMV only $146</li>
</ul>
<p>"I've tried several creative versions, but results are still poor." Xiao Li started doubting the product itself.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: It's Not the Product—It's the Creative Direction</h2>
<p>When our team took over the diagnosis, we found the problem wasn't the product itself.</p>
<p><strong>Core issue: Unclear creative direction led to无效曝光.</strong></p>
<p>The team was using static product images with factory narration—a typical "traditional trade export mindset": showcasing product specs and manufacturing capabilities. But TikTok users care about: How does this earphone sound? How does it look when worn? Is it worth it?</p>
<p>Problem breakdown:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Problem</th>
<th>Specific Issue</th>
<th>Impact</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Unclear creative direction</td>
<td>No testing on what users actually interested in</td>
<td>Budget wasted on无效曝光</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Single creative type</td>
<td>All factory-style explanation videos</td>
<td>High scroll-away rate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lacking trust signals</td>
<td>0 sales, 0 reviews, unpersuasive product page</td>
<td>High conversion decision barrier</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Judgment: Test Creative Direction First, Then Batch Produce</h2>
<p>TikTok Shop's traffic distribution is algorithm-driven—the system rapidly tests creatives, finds content that triggers engagement, and amplifies it.</p>
<p><strong>Key judgment: In cold start, creative direction matters more than quantity.</strong></p>
<p>Our strategy: spend a small budget to identify 2-3 promising creative directions, then quickly batch-produce around those directions.</p>
<h3>Creative Direction Planning</h3>
<p>Based on analysis of top-performing Bluetooth earphone content, we identified three possible directions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sound Quality Comparison</strong>: Use comparison tactics to showcase earphone sound quality (e.g., "Brand X vs. Ours"), creating auditory contrast</li>
<li><strong>Wear Demonstration</strong>: Real models, showing wearing effects in different scenarios, building usage immersion</li>
<li><strong>Urgency Promotion</strong>: Limited-time discounts, stock scarcity messaging, driving impulse conversion</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Actions: Building and Testing 20 Creatives</h2>
<h3>First Batch: 5-8 per direction</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Direction</th>
<th>Quantity</th>
<th>Specific Format</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Sound comparison</td>
<td>8</td>
<td>ASMR-style sound comparison, different music type tests</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Wear demonstration</td>
<td>7</td>
<td>Commute scenario, gym scenario, office scenario</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Urgency promotion</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>Countdown overlays, low-stock copy, exclusive coupon</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Production standards:</p>
<ul>
<li>Video length: 15-25 seconds (better completion rate)</li>
<li>First 3 seconds must have a hook: sound impact / visual contrast / question</li>
<li>No subtitles (users are习惯 viewing captions but auto-play may miss them)</li>
<li>Background music: Use TikTok trending BGM for native feel</li>
</ul>
<h3>Testing Rhythm</h3>
<p><strong>Days 1-2: Focus on Direction Testing</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>$50 daily budget</li>
<li>At least 2 creatives per direction</li>
<li>Monitor: CTR, add-to-cart rate, CVR</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Days 3-4: Data Elimination + Quick Iteration</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Keep creatives with CTR &gt; 2%</li>
<li>Pause underperformers</li>
<li>Quickly add new creatives around high-performing directions</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Days 5-7: Scale Top Creatives</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>80% budget to top 3 creatives</li>
<li>20% continues testing new directions</li>
<li>Monitor organic traffic conversion rate</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Results: Week-One ROI 1.5x, 32% Orders from Organic Traffic</h2>
<p>After 7 days, data comparison:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Before</th>
<th>After</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Ad spend</td>
<td>$182</td>
<td>$210</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>GMV</td>
<td>$146</td>
<td>$315</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR</td>
<td>0.7%</td>
<td>2.1%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROI</td>
<td>0.8</td>
<td>1.5</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Organic traffic %</td>
<td>8%</td>
<td>32%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>What happened?</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Sound quality comparison creatives had highest CTR (avg 2.4%), with one "airplane noise test" video driving 63% of GMV</li>
<li>Urgency promotion creatives converted fastest, but had higher cart abandonment</li>
<li>Wear demonstration creatives performed well on organic traffic, indicating strong "discovery" appeal</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Takeaways: 3 Key Insights for Creative Pool Building</h2>
<p><strong>1. Direction &gt; Quantity</strong></p>
<p>The biggest cold start mistake is "shooting a few random videos and boosting hard." Testing direction first then batching is the core logic for reducing trial-and-error costs.</p>
<p><strong>2. TikTok creatives need "content feel"</strong></p>
<p>Users encounter feed ads, not product detail pages. Creatives should feel like regular short videos that spark curiosity or resonance—not "buyer reviews + product specs."</p>
<p><strong>3. Creative pools need tiered management</strong></p>
<p>Organize creatives by direction for future iteration and new market reuse. Good creative directions can cross categories.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Next Step: How to Keep the Creative Pool Supplying?</h2>
<p>After successful cold start, this team's creative pool wasn't idle.</p>
<p>They migrated the proven "sound comparison" and "wear demonstration" directions to other product lines—Bluetooth speakers, wireless chargers. Testing cost dropped from $200 in the first week to $50 per product afterward.</p>
<p><strong>This is the real value of creative pool building: not one-time ad creatives, but sustainable content assets.</strong></p>
